Date: Mon, 25 Jan 93 22:48:36 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Patent law (was PPL)

Mark Read Pickens posts:

>In my mind, patents are distinctly separate from copyrights.  Patents
>involve the use of scientific principles which were not created by the
>inventor; they were discovered.  

This is not always the case, as one can get a patent for entirely whimsical
devices that do not apply scientific principles in any sort of
extraordinary way.  And patents that use scientific principles in an
obvious manner are not patentable under US law.  Nor can you get a patent
on only a scientific principle; it has to be embodied in some useful thing
or process.

>Multiple people frequently work on the same
>problem simultaneously, often arriving at identical solutions independently
>(and frequently without knowing of the other person's existance, let alone
>work).  Should the person who registered his invention first have special
>rights to the fruits of his labor; denying an equal right by the others to
>benefit from their own work?  

I'm not so confident that this happens frequently; I would like to see some
figures.  Also, please note that in the US primacy of registration *does
not* guarantee one's patent will prevail over nearly simultaneous
inventions.  If I have an adequate record of the conception of my idea and
work diligently on eventually patenting it, I can prevail against someone
who came up with a conflicting patent later, even if they patent their idea
first.


