Date: Mon, 25 Jan 93 22:48:20 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: PPL: Many Types of Intellectual Property Are Not Protected

Tim makes the good point that there are many areas where a lack of
intellectual property protection has not stopped innovation, that
doesn't really prove anything.  Perhaps there could be even more
innovation if intellectual property were protected in these fields.
What if Warhol got a royalty for each piece of modern art?  That many
more people would produce dazzling and original art, because it would
be that much easier to get rich.

The problem with having no patents is that you can invent something, some
major improvement in an industrial process, or a startling new gadget
(like the zipper), or whatever.  But you can't profit from it because
your competitors are able to copy what you've done.

Look at what happens in the drug business.  I am constantly reading that
drug companies won't test various natural substances because they are not
patentable, hence there is no opportunity to make profits to cover the
expensive testing phase.  (Granted, governments may make testing more
expensive than necessary, but I'm sure we all would prefer to buy well-tested
drugs even under PPL.)  Will anyone here dispute that removing patent
protection would slow the rate of new drug creation?

How much funding would a biotech startup get if it announced a policy
of not seeking patent protection for its discoveries?  They'd never get
off the ground.  There are just a lot of areas like this which are
expensive to enter but for which the products are cheap to produce and
easy to copy.  In those areas, patent protection will greatly speed the
rate of innovation and discovery.

Hal

