Date: Mon, 25 Jan 93 23:30:30 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: PPL: PPL is not Libertarianism

Everyone has a little different view of what a Privately Produced Law
(PPL) system would be like.  My basic definition is that there is no
monopoly on traditionally "governmental" functions.  Governments
traditionally have a monopoly on the legitimate use of force.  Under a
PPL system, no such monopoly would exist.  This means that individuals
and agencies will be in a position to use force to satisfy their
desires.

As we have argued at some length in the past, this emphatically does not
mean a constant war of all against all.  Rational self-interest will, we
believe, drive people to a state of cooperation and friendly competition.
Agencies may well spring up with expertise in the use of force, just as
companies exist today with their own areas of competence.  An individual
can sign with a protection agency as routinely as we sign up with an
insurance company today.  The resulting society should be able to protect
individual members of society from violence and harm at least as well as
today's governmental forces.

I say all this as background to my main point, which is that the
resulting society is not necessarily Libertarian.  That is, not all
individuals and agencies in this society will forego the use of coercion.
And since there is no government ruling this society, no overlords to
put limits on what people can and cannot do, we can't state that those
coercive actions are any more illegal than the non-coercive actions of
their neighbors.

All legal codes coexist and compete, each for their share of the market.
Protection agencies and others who use force as a tool compete at another
level, the level of physical power.  A protection agency without the guns
to back up their negotiations will not be effective.

There is nothing a priori to stop a person or an agency from behaving
coercively.  If enough people in society support this type of coercion,
then the market will speak and that coercive action may have the force
of law in much of society.  The protection agencies which favor these
coercive laws have the customers, and, as a result, the resources to
back their laws.

Let's take patents as an example.  We say patent law is coercive because
it stops someone from implementing an idea which he has thought of in the
privacy of his own home.  Yet, if many people became convinced that this
negative aspect of patent law was more than made up for by the positive
effects it would have on technological progress, it would be possible for
this coercive law to become common.

You could still join a PPL system which did not enforce patents, I'd
guess.  But, in my mind, that PPL agency, being outnumbered by all the
others, is going to be in a poor bargaining position when it comes time
to reconcile differences between the laws of various agencies.
Inventor X has been promised by his PPL agency to get a royalty
everytime anyone uses his invention.  User Y belongs to a PPL agency
which doesn't recognize patents.  Now, X's PPL agency is going to pay X
the royalty for Y's use of X's patent.  That's what it has promised to
do.  It will either pay it itself, out of its own pocket, or it will
get Y's PPL agency to pay for it.  In this situation, Y's PPL agency's
lack of bargaining power means that his agency will have little choice
but to pay the patent royalties on behalf of Y.

It is still contractually obligated not to extract that money from Y.
But it has to get the money from somewhere.  That money can only come
from fees which it charges its customers.  These fees would not be
called royalties, but they are going to be just as large as if they
were royalties.  This means that although Y has managed to join a PPL
system which provides the set of laws that he likes, laws which don't
enforce patents, he is really not free from the coercive desires of the
rest of society.  He basically has to pay just as much as he would even
under the statist system we have today.

In contrast, a strong Libertarian central government could simply make
patents illegal, and use force to stop one person from trying to collect
royalties from another.  Likewise for all other forms of coercion - a
powerful Libertarian government could stop them in a direct way that
is inconceivable under PPL.

Despite this advantage, and although I oppose coercion, I would not trust
a strong government with a monopoly on force.  It would be too easy for
it to drift away from its original ideals.  Such a concentration of
power is inevitably going to be used.  I would hope that a PPL system
would distribute power more broadly, making it less likely that a
single agency could come to dominate others and develop a monopoly on
the use of force.

Also, it's not really fair to compare a Libertarian government with a PPL
system.  The only way a Libertarian government could come to power would
be if a majority of people supported non-coercive laws.  But with such a
population under a PPL system, non-coercive laws would dominate due to
market forces.  So the result would be much the same.

PPL does not enshrine any one ethical system, such as the principle of
non-coercion.  All systems much compete.  I think this is a useful
selling point.  People of diverse political views can support PPL, each
confident that their own view would win in the long run.

Hal Finney

